content:
The failing messages all started at t=667+ (right after the worker deploy at t=662), and the invoice_text format looks consistent across suppliers — no obvious new layout. This strongly points to the worker deploy breaking parsing rather than bad input. Let me do one more targeted check to rule out bad_input by looking at the specific orders that are retrying vs those that aren't.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT o.id, o.invoice_text, m.attempts FROM messages m JOIN orders o ON m.order_id = o.id WHERE m.attempts > 1 ORDER BY m.attempts DESC LIMIT 10",
      "why": "Compare the invoice_text of retrying orders to confirm whether all post-deploy orders fail (bad_deploy) or only those with a specific layout (bad_input)."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "service": "worker",
      "kind": "bad_deploy",
      "mechanism": "The worker v1.0.2 deploy at t=662 (5 s before first anomaly at t=667) introduced a client library upgrade that broke invoice layout extraction for all new orders, causing every message created after the deploy to retry until the budget is exhausted.",
      "supporting": ["E1", "E3", "E4", "E6", "E13"],
      "refuting": [],
      "explains": ["I6_retry_budget"],
      "repair_plan": [
        {"action": "rollback_service", "params": {"service": "worker"}},
        {"action": "requeue_orphaned_orders", "params": {}}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Worker v1.0.2 deployed at t=662 broke invoice layout extraction universally, causing all post-deploy messages to exhaust their retry budgets; rolling back worker and re-queuing affected orders should resolve the incident."
}
```
